---
import DashboardLayout from '../layouts/DashboardLayout.astro';
import TopBar from '../components/TopBar.astro';
import ProsePage from '../components/ProsePage.astro';
import SearchModal from '../components/SearchModal.tsx';

export const prerender = true;
---

<DashboardLayout
  title="About Hivework"
  description="Hivework is an open-source registry and platform for discovering, inspecting and deploying AI components: agents, commands, skills, hooks, MCPs and settings."
>
  <TopBar />
  <ProsePage heading="About Hivework">
    <p>
      Hivework is an open-source registry for discovering, inspecting
      and installing components for AI agents and developer workflows: subagents, slash commands, skills, hooks, MCP server configurations,
      settings, loops and mods. It is maintained by <a href="https://github.com/allanindrajith">Allan Indrajith</a> together with
      community contributors, and every component lives in the public
      <a href="https://github.com/allanindrajith/Hivework">GitHub repository</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      Claude Code is configured through small, composable files, but good ones are scattered across personal repositories,
      gists and blog posts. There was no reliable way to find them, judge whether they are safe to run, or install them
      without copying files by hand. Hooks and MCP servers matter most here, because they run code with your local
      permissions. Component authors had the opposite problem: no neutral place to show their work to the people who would use it.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Web catalog.</strong> Every component has a page with its source, description, install command and a preview of the files it writes.</li>
      <li><strong>CLI.</strong> <code>npx claude-code-templates@latest</code> installs any component, or several at once, into the right project or user location and merges existing configuration instead of overwriting it.</li>
      <li><strong>Plain files.</strong> Components are not a black box. You can read, fork and edit them after installing, and each one links back to its source so changes can go upstream.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Security review</h2>
    <p>
      Every pull request that adds or changes a component goes through automated code review, a component validation
      pipeline and, for skills, the SkillSpector static security scanner. Components that execute code, such as hooks and
      MCP servers, get extra scrutiny of what they run and what they access. Still, read a component before you install it,
      especially one that runs commands.
    </p>

    <h2>Sponsored placements</h2>
    <p>
      Component authors and companies can pay for a sponsored placement: one component shown in a slot at the top of search
      results and as the first card of the component grid. That slot always carries a "Sponsored" label and the component
      passes the same review as any other. Payment never changes the order of the other results, the categories or the
      install recommendations. The revenue pays for hosting, security tooling and maintainer time.
    </p>

    <h2>Neutrality</h2>
    <p>
      The project is not a company and is not raising money. Components are judged on quality and safety, not on who built
      them or who paid. The code is MIT licensed.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Bugs, component requests and contributions go to
      <a href="https://github.com/allanindrajith/Hivework/issues">GitHub issues</a>. Security reports follow the
      <a href="https://github.com/allanindrajith/Hivework/security/policy">security policy</a>. For anything else,
      email <a href="mailto:allanindrajith@gmail.com">allanindrajith@gmail.com</a>.
    </p>
  </ProsePage>
  <SearchModal client:idle />
</DashboardLayout>
